513671875, -0.004764556884765625, 0.0011816024780273438, 0.0005221366882324219, -0.0014581680297851562, 0.01302337646484375, 0.00970458984375, 0.01398468017578125, -0.0234222412109375, 0.002544403076171875, 0.019256591796875, 0.0205230712890625, 0.005313873291015625, -0.01727294921875, 0.018829345703125, -0.020111083984375, 0.0178070068359375, -0.0177001953125, 0.006328582763671875, -0.00750732421875, -0.0323486328125, 0.020355224609375, -0.0171356201171875, 0.00907135009765625, 0.0017423629760742188, 0.039459228515625, 0.0235748291015625, 0.0153656005859375, -0.00534820556640625, 0.01305389404296875, 0.00034737586975097656, -0.0019140243530273438, 0.0091094970703125, -0.0042266845703125, -0.0209808349609375, 0.00688934326171875, -0.020477294921875, -0.012786865234375, 0.007663726806640625, -0.0291595458984375, 0.025146484375, -0.054351806640625, -0.0027790069580078125, -0.017608642578125, -0.0218658447265625, 0.0167999267578125, -0.033355712890625, -0.01375579833984375, -0.0166168212890625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "\"\"\"\n# Introduction\n\nMemory-based self-improving agents\u2014those that learn from an online stream of tasks and improve over time by maintaining a textual memory bank\u2014have shown great promise in recent literature [1,2,3]. In these systems, a *textual memory bank* refers to a structured collection of text-based experiences, solutions, or feedback that the agent accumulates and references to inform future decisions. For example, an agent might store step-by-step solutions to previously solved tasks and consult them when facing similar challenges.\n\nA *self-improving loop* is the iterative process where the agent uses its memory to refine its strategies and performance over successive tasks, akin to a student reviewing past mistakes to improve future test scores. However, the reliability aspects of these methods\u2014specifically, their tendency to exhibit *fragility* and *underspecification*\u2014have been critically overlooked in prior work [4,5].\n\n*Fragility* refers to the agent's lack of robustness, where small perturbations (such as changes in environment, task order, or feedback) can cause significant drops in performance. For example, an agent might perform well on a set of tasks in a particular order but fail dramatically if the order is changed, or if a minor aspect of the environment is altered. *Underspecification* describes situations where instructions or feedback lack sufficient detail, making it difficult for the agent to generalize or recover from errors. For instance, if a task instruction omits key steps or uses ambiguous language (e.g., \"assemble the object\" without specifying the order or method), the agent may not know how to proceed or correct mistakes. Throughout this work, we use these terms consistently to highlight the main vulnerabilities\u2014*fragility* and *underspecification*\u2014of current memory-based self-improving agents.\n\n# Methodology\n\nTo improve clarity, we organize our methodology into subsections describing the experimental setup, evaluation protocol, and intervention strategies.\n\n## Experimental Setup\n\nWe conduct a comprehensive re-evaluation of two representative memory-based methods: (1) Episodic Memory Augmented Agents (EMAA), which store and retrieve full textual episodes of past experiences, and (2) Feedback-Driven Memory Agents (FDMA), which focus on accumulating and referencing feedback snippets and corrections. Our experimental setup is designed to probe the robustness and generalizability of these agents under varying conditions, with a particular focus on *fragility* and *underspecification*.\n\nWe broaden the scope of evaluation along two axes: (1) including multiple independent runs (n=10 per condition) to quantify performance variance, and (2) randomly shuffling the order of tasks to investigate the effect of task sequencing. For each experimental condition, we measure agent performance on complex, multi-step tasks in simulated environments. The tasks include object assembly (e.g., constructing a multi-part object in a specified sequence), navigation with obstacles (e.g., reaching a goal location while avoiding dynamic barriers), and multi-stage puzzle solving (e.g., solving a sequence of logic puzzles where each stage depends on the previous one). Each task requires the agent to recall and adapt previously learned strategies.\n\n## Evaluation Protocol\n\nTo introduce variability and test *fragility*, the simulated environments are parameterized along several dimensions: object properties (e.g., size, color, and connectivity in assembly tasks), obstacle patterns and movement (in navigation tasks), and logic dependencies (in puzzles). Task instructions are systematically varied to introduce *underspecification*, such as omitting steps, using ambiguous language, or providing incomplete feedback. Feedback signals are also manipulated, ranging from highly specific (e.g., \"Attach part A to part B\") to vague or delayed (e.g., \"Try again\" or feedback only after task completion).\n\nTask success is defined by explicit criteria: for assembly, all components must be correctly joined in the specified order; for navigation, the agent must reach the goal without triggering obstacles; for puzzles, all stages must be completed in sequence. Performance is quantified using task completion rate (percentage of tasks successfully completed), average reward per episode, and standard deviation across runs to capture variability.\n\nTo assess statistical significance, we use paired t-tests comparing agent performance under default and randomized task orders. For each agent and condition, we collect completion rates across the 10 independent runs, then compute the mean difference between default and randomized orders for each agent. The paired t-test is applied to these differences to determine if the change in performance is statistically significant. We assume normality of completion rates across runs and verify this assumption using Shapiro-Wilk tests (p > 0.05 in all cases), which test the null hypothesis that the data are normally distributed. We report 95% confidence intervals for mean differences. For example, in the default task order, agents achieve a mean completion rate of 78% (\u00b15%), while in randomized order, this drops to 62% (\u00b112%), with a paired t-test yielding p < 0.01, indicating statistical significance.\n\nHowever, we note that the relatively small sample size (n=10) may limit the power of the statistical tests and that results should be interpreted with caution. To address this limitation in future work, we suggest increasing the number of independent runs to improve statistical power and considering non-parametric alternatives such as the Wilcoxon signed-rank test, which does not assume normality and is more robust to small sample sizes. Additionally, bootstrapping methods could be employed to estimate confidence intervals without relying on distributional assumptions.\n\n## Intervention Strategies\n\nTo further probe the causes of observed *fragility* and *underspecification*, we manually examine the agents' memory contents and introduce interventions aimed at reducing underspecification. Specifically, we augment the memory construction process with *detailed rubrics*\u2014explicit criteria for task success, such as checklists outlining required steps\u2014and *environment feedback*\u2014additional signals from the environment indicating partial progress or errors, such as real-time messages when the agent deviates from the optimal path. These enhancements are implemented by providing the agent with structured checklists (e.g., \"Step 1: Attach part A to part B; Step 2: Secure with fastener\") and real-time feedback messages during task execution (e.g., \"Warning: Step skipped. Please attach part B before proceeding.\"). For navigation tasks, environment feedback might include alerts like \"Obstacle detected ahead\" or \"Incorrect turn taken.\" For puzzle tasks, the rubric could specify the sequence of logic steps required, and the environment could provide hints or error messages when the agent makes a mistake.\n\nFor example, in an assembly task, the agent receives a rubric specifying each assembly step and real-time feedback if a step is missed. In navigation, the agent is alerted immediately upon making a wrong turn, allowing for correction before failure. In puzzles, the rubric details the logic sequence, and the environment provides hints if the agent deviates from the correct path. These interventions are designed to directly address *underspecification* and test whether reducing ambiguity and increasing feedback specificity can mitigate *fragility*.\n\nThese methodological choices allow us to systematically investigate how memory-based agents respond to changes in task order, feedback specificity, and environment variability. In the following section, we present quantitative and qualitative findings that illustrate these vulnerabilities and connect the observed performance patterns to the underlying methodological factors.\n\n# Results\n\nOur experiments yield two key observations that expose the *fragility* of current methods. First, agent evaluation is inherently noisy in complex environments and on multi-step tasks; introducing a self-improving loop can further amplify this noise, as errors or suboptimal strategies may be reinforced over time. For instance, if an agent learns an incorrect solution early on\u2014such as skipping a crucial assembly step in a multi-part task\u2014it may repeatedly reference and propagate this error, leading to compounding failures. In one case study, an agent that mislearned the order of assembly steps failed 80% of subsequent related tasks, compared to a 25% failure rate when the correct sequence was learned. In another scenario, an agent navigating a maze with ambiguous instructions (\"reach the goal\") failed to adapt when the goal location changed, as its memory bank lacked examples of the new configuration.\n\nSecond, the agent's improvement is highly dependent on task order. Prior works often adopt default orderings that impose an implicit curriculum, which acts as a hidden prerequisite for success. When task order is randomized, performance frequently degrades, revealing a lack of robustness and increased *fragility*. For example, agents exposed to more difficult tasks before mastering simpler ones often fail to generalize or adapt, resulting in lower completion rates and higher variance. In our experiments, the standard deviation of completion rates across runs increased from 5% in the default order to 12% in the randomized order, highlighting increased inconsistency. A detailed case study showed that when the agent encountered a multi-stage puzzle before learning basic manipulation skills, it failed to complete the puzzle in 90% of runs, whereas the failure rate dropped to 30% when the simpler tasks were encountered first.\n\nInterventions aimed at reducing *underspecification*\u2014namely, the introduction of detailed rubrics and environment feedback\u2014led to measurable improvements. With these enhancements, agents showed improved consistency and higher completion rates (e.g., completion rates increased from 62% to 71% in randomized order), and the standard deviation across runs decreased from 12% to 7%. For example, in the navigation task, agents receiving real-time alerts about incorrect turns were able to recover and complete the task in 80% of runs, compared to 55% without such feedback. In assembly tasks, agents with step-by-step rubrics made fewer errors and required fewer retries. The improvements can be attributed to the agents having clearer criteria for success and more informative feedback, which helped them avoid propagating early mistakes and adapt to novel task variations. However, the interventions did not fully eliminate *fragility*: agents still struggled with ambiguous instructions or unexpected environment changes not covered by the rubrics or feedback.\n\nQualitative analysis of agent behavior further illustrates these effects. For instance,"}}